&lt;p&gt;In Think deeper, every run we could inspect fits one rule: the price followed whether Rovo went off to read Jira or Confluence. Runs where it read nothing cost 10 to 26 as fresh messages. Runs where we saw it read site data cost 57 to 182.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I thought length would drive it. Pasting 2,000 words instead of 100 added about 15 credits. Asking for a 1,500-word plan got answers of about 2,000 words for 15 and 16 credits.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Steps aren't the meter either. The run with 75 steps cost 79, the one with 54 cost 94, and the one with 27 cost 182. On the 182 run Rovo did 5 Jira searches over 469 open items and named 33 of them in its answer. My guess is the charge follows how much text goes through the model while it reasons, retrieved data included. I can't prove it, and Atlassian doesn't publish a formula.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2Ff06182b30fd7b558bde4c97092c8720df3c97089-1440x1000.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2Ff06182b30fd7b558bde4c97092c8720df3c97089-1440x1000.png" title="The 182-credit message. Notice the repeated Searching Jira issues steps: Rovo deciding to read the whole project." alt="Rovo Chat in Think deeper mode on the question Look at all open work items in the Jira project SUPPORT, with the step panel open: Completed 27 steps, then Finding the right tools, Analyzing, Read Jira service guidance, Search all open work items in Jira project SUPPORT, three Searching Jira issues compactly steps, and an Analyzing note that there are 469 open issues" width="800" height="556"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our plain thread went 49, 44, 29, 26, and the Jira thread went 141, 86, 79, so later messages didn't get dearer. Follow-ups did cost a bit more than a fresh short prompt (26 to 44 against 10 to 16), which would fit a small charge for the context the thread carries. Three data points, so treat it as a hint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then the row that annoyed me most. On Let Rovo decide, the 469-item question cost 11 credits. That answer says it reviewed all 469 items, found the same six themes, counted the same 166 overdue items and named 26 work items. Its step panel says Completed 24 steps. On Think deeper the same question cost 106, 141 and 182. The work looked the same from where I sat. It's one run, so I won't call it a rule, but on that question the mode label was the difference between 11 and 182.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2F4f82eba9b6d2aad61e38f57efb9a398855aad50a-1600x896.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2F4f82eba9b6d2aad61e38f57efb9a398855aad50a-1600x896.png" title="Illustration, generated locally with ERNIE-Image. One coffee, and the waiter read the whole filing cabinet before bringing it. The bill comes after the coffee." alt="Cartoon of a stunned blue robot at a diner table holding a receipt for one coffee that runs across the floor, while a dusty silver robot waiter in a bow tie comes back from a back room of open filing cabinets" width="800" height="448"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Rovo credits pricing: Atlassian's own bands already allowed 300
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Atlassian's pages allowed for all of this. We misread them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://support.atlassian.com/rovo/docs/rovo-usage-limits/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;How Rovo credits work&lt;/a&gt; page lists Think deeper under "Variable credits per billable event" and names four things that move the number: "Reasoning steps", "Search volume", "Tools invoked" and "Model power". It also says Think deeper "dynamically plans, searches, reasons, and iterates to return a thorough answer, with built-in quality validation that automatically retries if it doesn't meet the quality bar." Atlassian's &lt;a href="https://www.atlassian.com/resources/usage" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;usage pricing page&lt;/a&gt; adds "the output generated", which our long-answer runs didn't bear out.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That support page also links an image called "Rovo Credits: Task Types". Light tasks are "Estimated Rovo Credits 10-200". Medium tasks are "201-500". Heavy tasks are "500+". The Wayback Machine's capture of 14 September doesn't have the image, and its capture of 17 September does. We quoted those bands in the first article and also wrote that Atlassian "publishes no number" for Think deeper. The second statement was wrong, and I should have caught it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So our 310 sits in Atlassian's own medium band, and a 300-plus call like the one we were told about is at least medium. The card has a heavy band above 500.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Rovo "will activate think deeper mode automatically if it detects a multi-layered or open-ended prompt", says the &lt;a href="https://support.atlassian.com/rovo/docs/compare-rovo-chat-reasoning-modes/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;reasoning modes page&lt;/a&gt;. Your users don't have to pick the expensive mode to get it. And Rovo shows no estimate before you send. &lt;a href="https://jira.atlassian.com/browse/ROVO-866" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ROVO-866&lt;/a&gt;, "Show estimated credit cost before submitting a prompt", had 6 votes on 27 October, and its description still quotes June's flat 10 and 100.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why this hurts on 3 December
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;From 3 December, extra usage is billed at "$0.01 per Rovo credit, or $10 per 1,000 credits", and "extra usage is on (by default)". A Jira Standard seat gets 25 credits a month. So our 182-credit Jira question is more than seven seat-months, and the 310 is twelve, or $3.10.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Your pool is shared, and you can't cap a person yet. The support page says "Coming soon: you'll be able to allocate Rovo credits by user", with no date. On &lt;a href="https://jira.atlassian.com/browse/ROVO-1224" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ROVO-1224&lt;/a&gt; a customer wrote on 26 October that one user spent 101,000 of their org's 196,000 credits in a month, and added: "Hearing there is no timeline for said feature feels nothing more like a cash grab." The broader request, &lt;a href="https://jira.atlassian.com/browse/ROVO-532" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ROVO-532&lt;/a&gt;, had 57 votes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You can't switch Think deeper off for Chat either. &lt;a href="https://jira.atlassian.com/browse/ROVO-1302" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ROVO-1302&lt;/a&gt; asks for control over the automatic switch (2 votes), and &lt;a href="https://jira.atlassian.com/browse/ROVO-1317" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ROVO-1317&lt;/a&gt; asks for an admin setting for the default mode (12 votes). And a usage limit won't stop an expensive message halfway: when a limit is hit, Atlassian says work already running completes and only new credit-consuming requests pause.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Afterwards you can't see which messages were the expensive kind. Our Features tab for the billing period that started 15 October, test included, shows "Rovo Chat 1,438 credits", every mode on one line. That's a different window from the export's 1,435, so the near match is a coincidence. The Teamwork Graph line stayed at 0 even though Rovo ran Teamwork Graph searches inside our chats.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2Fedc14588167f7df56a20c7478037e0aff742dc7f-1440x1100.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2Fedc14588167f7df56a20c7478037e0aff742dc7f-1440x1100.png" title="Our Features tab for the billing period that started 15 October, after the test. Chat messages sit on one Rovo Chat line whatever the mode." alt="Atlassian Administration, Meters, Rovo credits, Features tab for Oct 15 to Nov 15 2026 on our test org: Rovo Chat 1,438 credits, AI Slides 364, Rovo Agents 196, Self Service Agent 150, Teamwork Graph 0" width="800" height="611"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No quote before you send, no mode in the export, no cap per person. I can't budget that, and I doubt you can.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2F71657b5341739a16f3e64759823f94769a049228-1600x896.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fcdn.sanity.io%2Fimages%2F3oa2omis%2Fproduction%2F71657b5341739a16f3e64759823f94769a049228-1600x896.png" title="Illustration, generated locally with ERNIE-Image. The driver took the long way through your archives, and the receipt shows the total without the route." alt="Cartoon of a worried silver robot passenger clutching a wallet in the back of a yellow taxi while a grinning green robot driver steers through a maze of library shelves and the meter throws sparks" width="" height=""&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I'd do as an admin this week
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Export your CSV now and sort the Rovo Chat rows by Usage Count. If your top rows are in the hundreds, that's your real per-message price. The &lt;a href="https://leanzero.net/tutorials/check-rovo-credits-usage-each-user?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=crosspost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;export walkthrough&lt;/a&gt; shows where the button is, and the emailed link expires after 24 hours.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Tell people which questions cost money. Asking Rovo to read a project or a space ("look at all open items", "summarise every page") was our expensive class. Writing and rewriting stayed cheap even in Think deeper.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Try Let Rovo decide on the big read-everything questions before Think deeper. In our one run on the 469-item question it found the same themes and overdue count for 11 credits. One run proves little, but it's a cheap thing to test on your own org.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Set a usage limit before 3 December, or decide not to on purpose. The &lt;a href="https://leanzero.net/tutorials/rovo-usage-limit-extra-usage?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=crosspost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;usage limit walkthrough&lt;/a&gt; covers both settings.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Re-check any budget built on a fixed Think deeper price. Ours modelled it at 75.5 credits in the &lt;a href="https://leanzero.net/blog/atlassian-usage-based-pricing-budget-2027?utm_source=devto&amp;amp;utm_medium=referral&amp;amp;utm_campaign=crosspost" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;usage-based pricing budget&lt;/a&gt;, which is too low for data questions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Some of this we couldn't test. Deep Research has disappeared from the mode menu on our org, and the chat composer has no agent or model picker, so nothing here covers either. Our biggest Jira project has 469 open items and our biggest Confluence space has 13 pages. Yours are probably bigger, and my guess is that's how people get past 300.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What's the most expensive single Rovo message in your export? If you've got one above 310, I'd like to know what the question was.&lt;/p&gt;




